{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nOn arm64 macOS without Rosetta, 17 reconciler tests fail on a clean tree: `pypandoc_binary` 1.17's `macosx_11_0_arm64` wheel bundles an **x86_64** pandoc. uv picked the right wheel; its contents are wrong. Upstream [pypandoc#447](https://github.com/JessicaTegner/pypandoc/issues/447) is open, unassigned, no PR, and 1.17 is the newest release.\n\nReproduced at `2fb6062bfe5`, no local changes: `pytest tests/unit/reconciler/test_wiki_render.py tests/unit/reconciler/test_rich_text_cutover.py tests/unit/reconciler/diffing/test_diff_comments_cutover_neutral.py -q` → **17 failed, 57 passed**, `OSError: [Errno 86] Bad CPU type in executable`.\n\nTwo commits already landed for this defect class. `9af50584d0a` documents a **manual** download + `PYPANDOC_PANDOC` export (`docs/local-dev-env.md:35-66`); `fda55e3655e` widened provenance to accept the native arm64 binary (`scripts/generate_dc_wiki_legacy_outputs.py:65-72`). The remaining gap is making it **automatic**, so a fresh worktree is not born red.\n\n## Approach\n\nRepair at **bootstrap**, triggered by an **architecture mismatch** rather than a platform name or a CI provider.\n\n`scripts/ensure_pandoc.py` reads the first 16 bytes of the pandoc `pypandoc` bundles and decodes the architecture from the Mach-O or ELF header. Measured here: the bundled binary's header is `cffaedfe 07000001` (cputype `0x01000007` = `CPU_TYPE_X86_64`) while `platform.machine()` is `arm64`. When the decoded architecture matches the host the script exits 0 having touched no network — that is every Linux and x86_64-macOS environment, including all CI cells. On a mismatch it downloads the pinned upstream release, verifies the **extracted binary's** SHA-256 against a hard-coded pin, and installs it to the git-ignored `.tools/bin/pandoc`.\n\nThat shape is already in the repo: `Makefile:467-482` (`actionlint-bin`) installs a pinned, SHA-256-verified third-party binary into `.tools/bin` (`Makefile:51`). Reusing it adds no new mechanism kind, keeps the download out of `.venv` (where `uv sync` would overwrite it and falsify the wheel's recorded hashes), and never touches `PATH`.\n\nSelection must use `PYPANDOC_PANDOC`, not `PATH`: pypandoc probes every candidate and keeps the **highest** version it can execute, so a Homebrew pandoc 3.11 would beat a 3.9 in `.venv/bin` and break the `pandoc 3.9` pin assertion (`docs/local-dev-env.md:35-66` records this). So `tests/conftest.py` sets `PYPANDOC_PANDOC` to `.tools/bin/pandoc` when that file exists, the variable is unset, and the file's decoded architecture matches the host. When the file is absent the hook does nothing — no import, no work beyond one `exists()` — so CI behaviour is unchanged.\n\nTwo assumptions were tested before writing code. First, `PYPANDOC_PANDOC` alone is sufficient: exporting it in this worktree turns the command above into **74 passed** with the broken wheel binary left in place. Second, the upstream artifact is the one the repo already accepts: the member `pandoc-3.9-arm64/bin/pandoc` of `pandoc-3.9-arm64-macOS.zip` hashes to `140353e19f2518a76aa144ce90a7afcb6f485ba94d0dbe069e77c805277908c3`, which is already `ACCEPTED_PANDOC_BINARY_SHA256[\"darwin-arm64\"]` at `scripts/generate_dc_wiki_legacy_outputs.py:69`. A test asserts that membership so the fetched artifact cannot drift from the provenance allowlist.\n\nOn a mismatch the script cannot repair, `make install` **fails loudly** with the manual recipe in its message. Silent degradation is the defect this ticket exists to remove.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `scripts/ensure_pandoc.py` — new. Mach-O/ELF architecture decoding, the pinned download table, digest-verified install into `.tools/bin`, and `repo_pandoc_for_host()` for the test-time lookup.\n- `Makefile` — new `ensure-pandoc` target modelled on `actionlint-bin`; `install` invokes it after `uv sync`.\n- `tests/conftest.py` — set `PYPANDOC_PANDOC` from `.tools/bin/pandoc` when present and arch-matched. `_REPO_ROOT` already exists at line 115.\n- `tests/scripts/test_ensure_pandoc.py` — new regression tests. `tests/scripts/conftest.py:26-28` already puts `scripts/` on `sys.path`, so the module imports by bare name.\n- `docs/local-dev-env.md` — document the automatic path; keep the manual steps as the offline fallback.\n- `NOTICE` — record that the dev bootstrap may fetch the same pandoc 3.9 from `jgm/pandoc` releases into a git-ignored directory. Nothing is vendored or redistributed.\n\n## Testing\n\nIn `tests/scripts/test_ensure_pandoc.py` (marker `scripts`), with the network boundary injected — no test downloads anything:\n\n- **No-op branch:** synthetic Mach-O arm64 header on an `arm64` host. `ensure()` reports \"matches\" and never calls the injected downloader, which raises if called.\n- **Repair branch:** mismatched bundled binary plus an injected downloader returning bytes matching the pin. `ensure()` writes `.tools/bin/pandoc` and makes it executable.\n- **Failure path:** the injected downloader returns bytes whose digest does not match the pin. `ensure()` raises, and `.tools/bin/pandoc` does not exist afterwards. A silently-accepted bad download would reintroduce an unrunnable binary.\n- **Edge paths:** `read_binary_arch` decodes Mach-O `cffaedfe 07000001` → `x86_64`, Mach-O `cffaedfe 0c000001` → `arm64`, ELF `e_machine` `0x3e` → `x86_64`, ELF `0xb7` → `arm64`, and returns `None` for a short or unknown file, so an unrecognised format degrades to \"no mismatch\", never to a spurious download.\n- **Absent/mismatched path:** `repo_pandoc_for_host()` returns `None` when `.tools/bin/pandoc` is absent, and when it is present but its decoded architecture differs from the host.\n- **Pin-drift guard:** the digest `ensure_pandoc.py` pins is a member of `ACCEPTED_PANDOC_BINARY_SHA256[\"darwin-arm64\"]`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] After `make install` on this arm64 macOS host, `file .tools/bin/pandoc` reports `Mach-O 64-bit executable arm64`.\n- [ ] `.tools/bin/pandoc --version | head -1` reports `pandoc 3.9`.\n- [ ] With no `PYPANDOC_PANDOC` exported and no manual step, `pytest tests/unit/reconciler/test_wiki_render.py tests/unit/reconciler/test_rich_text_cutover.py tests/unit/reconciler/diffing/test_diff_comments_cutover_neutral.py -q` reports **74 passed** (it reports 17 failed, 57 passed today).\n- [ ] With `.tools/bin/pandoc` deleted, `test_pypandoc_and_pandoc_versions_are_pinned` FAILS rather than skips; checked by running that node and reading its outcome.\n- [ ] `python scripts/ensure_pandoc.py` exits 0 and performs no network access when the decoded architecture matches the host; checked by the no-op-branch test whose injected downloader raises if called.\n- [ ] `tests/conftest.py` sets no environment variable when `.tools/bin/pandoc` is absent; checked by the `repo_pandoc_for_host()` absent-path test.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/scripts/test_ensure_pandoc.py -q` reports all tests passing, including the bad-digest case.\n- [ ] `make verify` from this worktree shows zero failures in `tests/unit/reconciler/`; the only remaining failures on this hardware are the 22 `tests/scripts/test_observability_*` failures owned by `7f3d-ddc9-04d9-4861`. (The original \"`make verify` exits 0\" criterion is unreachable by this ticket alone — see the comment of 2026-10-27.)\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exits 0."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793213046633683112,"uuid":"e42f2246-d4ad-4f47-b511-20e8de73ba2b"}